\subsection{Motivation and Background}

\head{Home Monitoring.} A home monitoring system is a network of interconnected devices and software designed to observe, analyze, and respond to in-home activities. Traditional solutions typically rely on cameras, motion detectors, and door/window sensors to detect anomalies. This supports security and energy efficiency by triggering alarms, controlling HVAC systems, and managing lighting. As such systems become increasingly prevalent, three main needs emerge: robust protection against intrusions and emergencies; support for aging populations to enable independent living; and energy conservation through occupancy-based control.

Modern systems extend far beyond basic intrusion detection, offering real-time security alerts with smart locks, automated energy usage through HVAC/lighting controls, ambient assisted living for vulnerable groups, non-intrusive child/pet monitoring, and prevention of fires or leaks. As these systems evolve, so do user expectations. Today’s home monitoring solutions must preserve privacy, integrate seamlessly with existing smart home ecosystems, be affordable, and remain reliable across diverse conditions (\textit{e.g.}, varying lighting, through-wall detection, and multiple occupants).



\head{WiFi for Home Monitoring.} WiFi-based monitoring offers notable advantages over traditional sensor-based approaches. While cameras and microphones provide rich detail~\cite{camera_CHI,camera_IPSN,camera_PMC,camera_IEEEP}, they often raise privacy concerns and may be unsuitable for continuous surveillance~\cite{sound_privacy,camera_privacy_concern1,camera_privacy_concern2,camera_privacy_concern3,camera_privacy_concern4}. Wearables~\cite{wearables_JSAC,wearables_TBE,wearables_TITB} require users to carry and charge devices and typically cover only the wearer~\cite{wearables_less_use1,wearables_less_use2}. Although mmWave radars can achieve fine-grained motion tracking~\cite{mmwave_CST,mmwave_INFOCOM,mmwave_IOTJ}, they introduce extra costs, require complex installation, and generally demand line-of-sight (LOS). Passive infrared (PIR) sensors~\cite{pir_0,pir_1,pir_2,pir_3} are inexpensive and simple to deploy, but only detect coarse motion and also depend on LOS, limiting their sensing accuracy and range. Environmental factors such as temperature and airflow may further trigger false alarms~\cite{pir_false_alarm}. We summarize popular modalities in existing market and literature~\cite{home_monitoring_survey1, home_monitoring_survey2, home_monitoring_survey3} in Table \ref{tab:ModalityComparison}.

By contrast, WiFi-based sensing leverages the ubiquity of wireless networks to enable contactless motion detection throughout the home using existing infrastructure. This approach is cost-effective, relatively unobtrusive, and can be scaled widely with minimal modification, making it a practical and privacy-conscious option for home monitoring applications.

The main research goal of a WiFi-based home monitoring system is to exploit standard, omnipresent WiFi infrastructure to unobtrusively and cost-effectively detect, localize, and analyze occupant presence and activity. However, this is challenged by inherent multipath propagation, which obscures motion-related signal features, and commodity hardware limitations, including intrinsic frequency/phase offsets and time-varying distortions that undermine sensing accuracy. These issues often necessitate elaborate calibration procedures or specialized hardware modifications that are not always feasible for widespread residential deployment.

% WiFi sensing leverages existing infrastructure for contactless motion detection, providing a cost-effective, unobtrusive, and scalable solution. It provides a practical and privacy-conscious alternative for a wide array of home monitoring applications, from elder care to general security and energy management.

% The main research goal of a WiFi-based home monitoring system is to leverage existing, ubiquitous WiFi infrastructure to unobtrusively and cost-effectively detect, locate, and analyze occupant presence and activity. However, developing a well-rounded WiFi-based home monitoring system is inherently challenging due to the physical and hardware constraints of standard wireless networks. Multipath signal propagation complicates feature isolation, while off-the-shelf hardware introduces signal distortions (\textit{e.g.}, frequency/phase offsets), necessitating calibration or hardware modifications impractical for residential use.
