% This section articulates how real-world constraints and user requirements led us to a design that is (i) calibration-free, (ii) capable of through-the-wall detection in complex indoor environments, and (iii) robust to the hardware synchronization issues that corrupt raw CSI phase. We talk about each user expectations, practical challenges, and our strategies to overcome them, and conclude with a overview of the system’s core modules. 

\head{Calibration-Free.} A calibration-free approach is essential for non-technical users. Our system leverages the statistical properties of electromagnetic (EM) wave propagation to eliminate environment-specific calibration~\cite{Hill2009}. Under static conditions, the power response of the CSI at time $t$ of the subcarrier $f$, denoted \( G(t,f) = |H(t,f)|^2 \), follows a Gaussian distribution:
$
G(t,f) \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu(f), \sigma^2(f)),
$
where \(\mu(f)\) and \(\sigma^2(f)\) are the mean and variance of the static channel. Human motion perturbs the multipath structure, introducing temporal correlation in \( G(t,f) \). This effect is captured by the \textit{autocorrelation function} (ACF):
$$
\rho_G(\tau,f) = \frac{\mathbb{E}[G(t,f)G(t-\tau,f)] - \mu^2(f)}{\sigma^2(f)},
$$
where \(\tau\) is the time lag. In static environments, \(\rho_G(\tau,f)\) approaches zero for \(\tau \neq 0\). By contrast, motion causes \(\rho_G(\tau,f) > 0\). We define a \textit{motion statistic} (MS) \(\phi(f) = \rho_G(\Delta t,f)\), with \(\tau=\Delta t\) as the sampling interval. The system detects motion through hypothesis testing~\cite{widetect}:
$$
\mathcal{H}_1: \phi(f) > \eta ~ \text{(Motion detected)}, ~ \mathcal{H}_0: \phi(f) \leq \eta ~ \text{(Static)},
$$
where \(\eta\) is a universal threshold determined empirically with a theoretical basis, and no environment-specific calibration is required~\cite{widetect}.

\head{Through-the-Wall.}
To ensure whole-home coverage with minimal devices, the system must detect motion behind walls or in distant rooms. Instead of relying on a dominant reflection path, we harness the cumulative energy of all multipath components in indoor environments. The ACF for non-line-of-sight (NLOS) motion is modeled as:
$$
\rho_G(\tau) = \frac{1}{F} \sum_{f=1}^F \frac{E_d^2(f)}{E_d^2(f) + \sigma^2(f)} \cdot J_0(kv\tau),
$$
where \(E_d^2(f)\) is the motion-induced energy, \(J_0\) is the zeroth-order Bessel function~\cite{Weisstein2002}, \(k = 2\pi/\lambda\) is the wavenumber, and \(v\) is the target speed. The first peak \(\tau_{\text{peak}}\) in the differential of ACF \(\Delta\rho_G(\tau)\) reveals the speed: 
$
v = \frac{x_0\lambda}{2\pi\tau_{\text{peak}}}, 
$
where $x_0$ is the first peak in the original $J_0(x)$.
To amplify weak NLOS components, the system applies maximal ratio combining (MRC)~\cite{Shumway2011,Sklar2001}:
$
S(\tau) = \sum_{f=1}^F w(f) \rho_G(\tau,f), w(f) \propto \rho_G(\Delta t,f).
$
By emphasizing subcarriers with stronger motion signatures, MRC improves the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) by 8–12 dB, enabling speed estimates beyond 10 m, even through walls.

\head{Handling Complex Home Environments.}
Indoor settings are characterized by dense multipath propagation, which will complicate sensing for many prior methods. Differently, our design leverages multipath diversity to improve sensitivity: as the number of perturbed paths \(N\) increases, the variance of \(\rho_G(\tau)\) diminishes, as \(\text{Var}(\rho_G(\tau)) \propto 1/N\)~\cite{gaitway}. Consequently, richer multipath environments inherently reduce noise and bolster detection robustness.


\head{Phase-Agnostic.}
Commodity WiFi devices often exhibit time-varying phase distortions—such as carrier frequency offset (CFO) and sampling frequency offset (SFO)—that corrupt raw CSI phase data~\cite{xie2015precise,Chen2017}. By using ACF, a second-order statistic synchronized over all subcarriers~\cite{gaitway}, our system avoids dependence on the raw phase. Instead, it relies on CSI power \(|H(t,f)|^2\) only, which remains immune to phase errors. This approach achieves motion detection accuracy on par with phase-based methods, ensuring reliability across diverse hardware.

