\subsection{System Overview}
Building on the foundation module, we design a comprehensive WiFi-based home monitoring system addressing the practical deployment challenges of moving subject recognition, inconsistent sensing measurements, multi-user sensing, and edge deployment. 
Our problem-driven design addresses many issues commonly faced by WiFi sensing systems and generally achieves robust performance in typical conditions. However, while deploying our technology on diverse commercial edge devices across millions of households, we uncover additional technical and practical barriers that hinder the broader adoption and reliability of WiFi-based monitoring, as discussed previously. 
In the following, we present an overview of our system design (as illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:sys-overview}), and propose solutions to these barriers in \S\ref{sec:sub} to \S\ref{sec:computation}. 

% Figure \ref{fig:sys-overview} illustrates the overview of our system. 

On the \textbf{edge side}, the CSI capture front end runs on an embedded platform linked to a standard access point (AP), sampling packets at 100 Hz. The device driver extracts the complex CSI across all subcarriers, yielding \(|H(t,f)|\) for \(f \in F\).
A \textbf{quality check module (\S\ref{sec:quality})} then filters out low-quality data, and the resulting high-quality CSI is passed to the \textbf{foundation module (\S\ref{sec:foundation})}, which maintains a rolling buffer of the past \(T\) seconds’ CSI data. Within this module, we first extract amplitude-based signals \(|H(t,f)|^2\) and compute the ACF and MS with MRC. Motion detection and speed estimation are then performed based on MS and ACF, respectively. The module also maintains a short history of ACF results. A \textbf{hybrid mechanism (\S\ref{sec:computation})} then decides whether to offload this ACF queue to the cloud for subject recognition. 

On the \textbf{cloud side}, the \textbf{subject recognition module (\S\ref{sec:sub})} in the sensing server classifies the subject based on features extracted from the ACF queue. The decision is stored in a database and relayed through a front server to the user interface (UI). The interface presents real-time notifications of motion detection and location, historical activity summaries, and device management tools. Users can track ongoing motion or presence detections, examine performance metrics such as sensing quality and data throughput, and statistics such as motion strength, speed estimations and gait features.
