\head{Approaches.}
The key limitation stems from treating all motion as a monolithic phenomenon, disregarding distinct movement mechanics—bipedal strides for humans, quadrupedal gaits for pets, and continuous wheel rotations for robots.
Inspired by~\cite{wi_moid, Guozhen_conference_version}, we realize that movement mechanics—not just speed or intensity—enable reliable discrimination. Humans exhibit a \textit{bipedal} stance–swing cycle, pets move \textit{quadrupedally}, and wheeled robots maintain relatively consistent speeds. Fig. \ref{fig:speed_pattern} shows the CSI amplitude and corresponding speed estimates for a human, a dog, and an iRobot. Even when speeds partially overlap (\textit{e.g.}, a small dog running as fast as a human), stride length and cycle time remain distinct (Fig. \ref{fig:gait_cycle}). Meanwhile, vacuum robots exhibit smooth, linear speed profiles punctuated by abrupt changes on collision, clearly contrasting with the rhythmic legged locomotion.

