Building on this insight, we extract physical features through speed patterns and analyze the consistency of environment-agnostic statistics. Specifically, we extract 7 physical features, including the existence of gait, stride length, stride cycle time, average speed, speed variance, speed 25 percentile, and speed 75 percentile. Furthermore, 6 statistical features are considered, including ACF peak mean, ACF valley mean, ACF peak interval distance, ACF valley interval distance, motion statistic mean, and motion statistic variance. We employ an SVM classifier to identify the motion from the subject of interest based on the extracted features. 

\head{Experiments.}
To validate this approach, we collect over 2.4 million data samples spanning 84 days in 7 homes, from small apartments to single-family houses, with each sample representing a 6-second window. We label motion data for non-human subjects in situations where no humans are present, aiming to distinguish such events from human motion. During testing, we make no model adjustments or classifier retraining for any unseen environment. Our dataset captures everyday human activities, pets of varying sizes (5 dogs and 4 cats ranging from 7.5 lb to 50 lb), robotic vacuums under normal household use, and oscillating or ceiling fans. All subjects move freely, often behind walls or furniture, ensuring realistic conditions.
