\subsection{Deployment Considerations}
\head{Scalability:} The hierarchical device architecture (Bot → Origin → Master Origin) allows the system to expand in environments with multiple WiFi APs or dense device deployments. Each MO can handle multiple Origins without saturating a single processing node.

\head{Fault Tolerance:} If an MO becomes unavailable, another capable Origin may step in temporarily as the MO, reducing downtime while ensuring quality of service (QoS).


\head{Local vs. Cloud Processing:} Foundation modules run on local devices to reduce backhaul network load. Advanced inference may be local (on the MO) or partially migrated to the cloud if remote computational resources are preferred.


\head{Data Protection:} By ensuring that the SS does not store personal user information, the system isolates high-level user data to the FS, simplifying data protection. TLS-based MQTT sessions safeguard transmissions across the internet.

\head{WiFi Sensing Privacy and Security:}
Although WiFi sensing generally raises fewer privacy concerns than video or audio streams, unauthorized access to raw CSI could still reveal sensitive information such as user location patterns or daily activities~\cite{privacyLocation2020}. Likewise, physical-layer attacks—\textit{e.g.}, signal jamming or adversarial interference—and cloud-layer exploits pose security risks.
While encryption and authentication can address many cloud-based threats, mitigating CSI-level vulnerabilities (\textit{e.g.}, adversarial attacks on WiFi preambles~\cite{Li2024}, covert tampering via reconfigurable intelligent surfaces~\cite{RIStealth}) remains challenging. Approaches like CSI fuzzing~\cite{Zhang2024Privacy} and channel hopping~\cite{djuraev2022channel} show promise. 

Despite progress in WiFi sensing security, current approaches face challenges including limited device-level control, performance–security trade-offs, and inconsistent standards. These obstacles, alongside an evolving threat landscape, underscore the need for adaptive privacy-preserving techniques that fit within commodity hardware constraints. Future collaboration among device manufacturers, researchers, and standards bodies could refine efficient, unified defenses while maintaining system usability.

% Although cloud-related threats can generally be managed through established encryption and authentication methods, mitigating risks at the CSI level remains challenging due to limited practical solutions. Recent studies have demonstrated vulnerabilities such as adversarial attacks manipulating WiFi packet preambles~\cite{Li2024} and covert attacks utilizing reconfigurable intelligent surfaces~\cite{RIStealth}. Proposed defensive strategies include CSI fuzzing~\cite{Zhang2024Privacy} and channel hopping approaches~\cite{djuraev2022channel}.

