We elaborate on the experimental setting in \Cref{sec:exp_setting}, and present the results that include overall segmentation performance, time complexity, space complexity, class-wise IoU performance, and qualitative visualization in \Cref{sec:exp_results}. Moreover, we ablate different projection rays, design choices, and sensitivity of parameters in \Cref{sec:exp_ablation}. 



\subsection{Experimental Setting}
\label{sec:exp_setting}




\subsubsection{Datasets and Evaluation Metrics} We conduct experiments on 3D scene datasets, S3DIS~\cite{armeni2017joint} and ScanNet~\cite{dai2017scannet}. S3DIS covers $271$ rooms in $6$ areas with $13$ classes, where Area 5 is for inference and others are for training. ScanNet contains $1613$ cluttered indoor scenes annotated with $20$ classes, which are split into $1201$ training scans, $312$ validation scans, and $100$ test scans. We evaluate our methods on three metrics: OA ($\%$), mACC ($\%$), and mIoU ($\%$). OA is the overall accuracy, mACC is the mean accuracy within each class, and mIoU is the mean intersection over union.



\subsubsection{Model Architectures} Our framework of VDP has MLP feature channel numbers as $2$, $9$, and $1$ with Sigmoid activation. We set input resolution of projected images as $224$, and the following resolutions are $56$, $28$, and $14$. To ingest projected images, we adopt $3$ layers of Swin-T~\cite{liu2021swin} as the 2D pre-trained backbone, where it is pre-trained on ImageNet-1K~\cite{deng2009imagenet} with $27.5$M fixed parameters. The feature pyramid network (FPN) generates feature maps, involving input channels as $192$, $384$, and $768$, and output channels aligning with encoder channels. We adopt the same segmentation backbone in MirageRoom~\cite{sun2024mirageroom}, which has $3$ encoder layers with channel numbers as $96$, $192$, and $384$. The 2D pre-trained backbone of our approach is Swin-T~\cite{liu2021swin}, and we further substitute it with a lightweight SG-Former-S~\cite{ren2023sg}. SG-Former-S~\cite{ren2023sg} has $21.8$M fixed parameters and changes the FPN input channels to $64$, $128$ and $256$, from which we use a superscript $^{(+SG)}$ for discrimination. Therefore, it is worth noting that we separately conduct $2$ groups of comparisons: the proposed PointVDP and the backbone MirageRoom based on Swin-T~\cite{liu2021swin}, and the proposed PointVDP$^{(+SG)}$ and the backbone MirageRoom$^{(+SG)}$ based on SG-Former-S~\cite{ren2023sg}. 




\subsubsection{Implementation Details} All experiments are conducted on a $24$G RTX $3090$ GPU with the PyTorch toolkit. We perform training processes for $100$ epochs through the AdamW optimizer with a learning rate of $0.006$. The weight decay is $0.05$ for both datasets. We set the number of view planes $M$ as $4$ for each scene to generate fireworks rays. We retrain the baseline using $\kappa \in \{0,1,2\}$ to align with the original settings of VIP to project images. For the framework of VDP in the proposed PointVDP, we set the number of central points $S$ of FPS as $32$, the radius $r$ of BQ as $0.2$, and the weight $\omega$ of attention as $0.8$. The upper and lower bounds are $\kappa_a$ as $5$ and $\kappa_b$ as $-5$ for mutation. The tuning parameter $\tau$ is $0.8$ for S3DIS and $0.4$ for ScanNet. The balanced weight $\lambda$ of loss is $0.2$ for S3DIS and $0.3$ for ScanNet.





\subsection{Results}
\label{sec:exp_results}



\subsubsection{Semantic Segmentation Results on S3DIS and ScanNet}

The baseline is reported using four $48$G GPUs, achieving OA/mAcc/mIoU as $91.3\%$/$78.2\%$/$72.0\%$ on S3DIS and mIou as $74.9\%$ on ScanNet. For fair comparisons, we retrain the baseline and our proposed method using a $24$G GPU. We show the results of our method and recent state-of-the-art methods in \Cref{tab:exp_miou}. As the model architectures explained in \Cref{sec:exp_setting}, our method depicts the improved segmentation results of the proposed PointVDP and PointVDP$^{(+SG)}$ compared with the baseline MirageRoom and MirageRoom$^{(+SG)}$, where the superscript $^{(+SG)}$ means a different 2D re-trained backbone. It is important to note that the 2D pre-trained backbone used in $^{(+SG)}$ is more lightweight with a higher classification result, where SG-Former-S~\cite{ren2023sg} achieves 83.2$\%$ and Swin-T~\cite{liu2021swin} achieves 81.2$\%$ on ImageNet-1K~\cite{deng2009imagenet}. Based on the advantage, our PointVDP$^{(+SG)}$ significantly outperforms our PointVDP for the 3D semantic segmentation task on S3DIS and ScanNet. We compare the baseline and our proposed method from $2$ groups within the same backbones. \textit{First}, PointVDP outperforms MirageRoom with $2.8\%$/$5.7\%$/$6.0\%$ in terms of the OA/mAcc/mIoU on the test set Area 5 of S3DIS dataset. We also report mIoU on the validation set of ScanNet. Our PointVDP improves MirageRoom by $9.3\%$. These results indicate that our proposed VDP method provides more informative projections compared with the existing VIP method. \textit{Second}, for S3DIS dataset, our PointVDP$^{(+SG)}$ further achieves competitive performances on all three metrics as $90.7\%$, $76.5\%$, and $71.0\%$ with small amounts of learnable parameters compared with other existing methods. By using limited computing resources, our PointVDP$^{(+SG)}$ achieves $72.1\%$, which outperforms MirageRoom$^{(+SG)}$ and completes promising segmentation. Based on a reduced number of parameters as $5.6$M, our method outperforms many recently proposed methods. 


\subsubsection{Time Complexity and Space Complexity} We outline the time complexity and space complexity of the baseline models and the proposed models in~\Cref{tab:exp_batch}, and all efficiency metrics are measured across the S3DIS dataset. We compare $2$ different 2D pre-trained backbones for $4$ methods, including MirageRoom, PointVDP, MirageRoom$^{(+SG)}$, and PointVDP$^{(+SG)}$. For time complexity, we calculate the time usage and speed to evaluate the model efficiency during the training and inference procedures, and our approach maintains reasonable efficiency. For space complexity, we report memory consumption, the maximum batch size, and the number of 2D projected images. More images introduce more computations, where the baseline constructs $12$ images per scene. We generate $4$ optimal images, thereby achieving a decrease of $1-(4/12)=66.7\%$. The decreased image number directly leads to an increased batch size. Our training process on the original baseline is finished with a maximum batch size of $1$. We achieve dynamically diverse projections by PointVDP, so the demands of projected images are reduced with an increased batch size of $3$. Then, we substitute a lightweight 2D pre-trained backbone to increase the batch size of baseline to $3$ and PointVDP$^{(+SG)}$ to $6$. Besides, we compare the model size and GPU demand. The previous method is conducted on $4\times48$G GPUs, and our lightweight method uses $1\times24$G GPU. Thus, the computation cost is reduced by $1-(24/(4\times48))=87.5\%$. As a result, our method reduces projected images by $66.7\%$, which achieves lightweight input for 2D feature processing. Moreover, we reduce the computation costs by $87.5\%$ and achieve comparable results compared with the original baseline method.




\subsubsection{Class-wise IoU Performance}

Based on overall mIoU results, we thoroughly analyze class-wise IoU results of S3DIS in~\Cref{tab:supp_per_class_s3dis} and ScanNet in~\Cref{tab:supp_per_class_scannet}. We evaluate our proposed method and the baseline, comparing PointVDP with MirageRoom, and PointVDP$^{(+SG)}$ with MirageRoom$^{(+SG)}$. Our method achieves higher IoUs on most semantic classes, where PointVDP$^{(+SG)}$ has the best results on $10$ out of $13$ semantic classes of S3DIS and $17$ out of $20$ semantic classes of ScanNet. We demonstrate performance improvements, declines, or constants of our methods compared with the baseline. For example, PointVDP$^{(+SG)}$ shows $10$ best results on S3DIS including \textit{wall} ($\uparrow 1.5\%$), \textit{beam} ($\uparrow 0.3\%$), \textit{column} ($\uparrow 0.5\%$), \textit{window} ($\uparrow 8.1\%$), \textit{door} ($\uparrow 6.9\%$), \textit{table} ($\uparrow 1.7\%$), \textit{chair} ($\uparrow 1.8\%$), \textit{sofa} ($\uparrow 9.0\%$), \textit{bookcase} ($\uparrow 2.3\%$), and \textit{board} ($\uparrow 5.5\%$). Meanwhile, compared with MirageRoom$^{(+SG)}$, PointVDP$^{(+SG)}$ has improvements on \textit{table} ($\uparrow 1.2\%$), \textit{bkshf} ($\uparrow 2.8\%$), \textit{fridge} ($\uparrow 4.9\%$), \textit{sink} ($\uparrow 3.3\%$), and many other classes for ScanNet. We conclude that the class-wise improvements are consistent in different dataset selections.




\subsubsection{Qualitative Visualization} 
To provide intuitive comparisons, we visualize semantic segmentation results on challenging scenes. \Cref{fig:exp} and~\Cref{fig:supp_exp} present the performances
of S3DIS and ScanNet datasets. It is important to note that our comparisons are based on the $^{(+SG)}$ backbone. Starting from the leftmost figure and moving to the right, the results contain the visualizations of the input scene, baseline, proposed method, and GroundTruth results. Taking the real color and semantic color as references, we also provide the related 2D image projection from the inner part of each 3D scene. We use the yellow dashed circles to highlight our improvements. Compared with the baseline, our method performs better with a leading performance, which also gradually aligns with the GroundTruth result. For example, our method boosts the segmentation on \textit{wall}, \textit{door}, and \textit{bookcase} for S3DIS, as well as \textit{wall}, \textit{bed}, \textit{wind}, and \textit{desk} for ScanNet. These improvements validate the effectiveness of our method, and we achieve better or more comparative results on challenging benchmarks.





