While every disconnected graph obviously has a $2$-flexible realization,
it has a NAC-coloring if and only if it has at least two components which are not isolated vertices
or it has a component that has a NAC-coloring.

It was proven that the existence of a NAC-coloring for a general graph is
NP-complete by a reduction from the 3-SAT~\cite{np_complete}.
There is a related NP-complete problem, namely, the existence of a stable cut,
where a \emph{stable cut} $S$ of a graph $G$ is a vertex cut, i.e., $G - S$ is disconnected,
that is stable (independent), i.e., no two vertices in $S$ are adjacent.
The relation is the following:
if a graph has a stable cut, it has also
a NAC-coloring~\cite[Theorem 4.4]{GLS2019}, but not the other way around,
since for instance the $3$-prism graph does not have a stable cut.
The existence of a stable cut
is NP-complete even on the class of graphs with maximum degree five~\cite{stable_cuts}
and on $n$-vertex graphs with at most $(2+\varepsilon)n$ edges~\cite{Le2003}.
For a summary of the complexity results on stable cuts and FPT algorithms, see also~\cite{RRS2023}.
Our first main result is the proof that the existence of a NAC-coloring,
and hence also of a $2$-dimensional flexible realization,
is NP-complete on the class of graphs with maximum degree five
and on $n$-vertex graphs with at most $(2+\varepsilon)n$ edges.

From now on we focus only on $d=2$, hence, we use rigid, resp.\ flexible, instead of $2$-rigid, resp. $2$-flexible.

When studying paradoxical flexibility of a graph $G$,
the knowledge of $\nac{G}$ might provide more information about the possible flexible realizations of $G$
than just the existence.
A \emph{motion} is an irreducible algebraic curve of realizations containing a nontrivial flex.
To each motion, one can assign a certain set of NAC-colorings of the underlying graph,
called the \emph{active NAC-colorings} of the motion, see~\cite[Definition~1.6]{GLSclassification}.
The construction used in the proof of \Cref{thm:NACmain} shows that for every NAC-coloring~$c$,
there is a motion whose set of active NAC-colorings is exactly $\{c\}$.
On the other hand, a motion can have more than one active NAC-coloring.
For instance, for the motion corresponding to one of the flexible realisation of $K_{3,3}$ constructed by Dixon,
all NAC-colorings of~$K_{3,3}$ are active~\cite[Example~4.3]{GLSclassification}.
An active NAC-coloring might enforce some algebraic constrains on the distances between adjacent vertices,
which can be used for some small graphs to classify all possible motions, see~\cite{GLSclassification}.

Motivated by the above, one might want to compute all NAC-colorings besides checking the existence.
The package \flexrilog~\cite{flexrilog} can be used, but it is rather slow.
It exploits the following concept: let $\triangle$ be a relation on the edge set of a graph $G$
such that $e_1 \triangle e_2$ if and only if there exists a 3-cycle $C$ in $G$
such that $e_1, e_2 \in E(C)$.
The graph $G$ is \trcon{} if $E(G)$ is the only equivalence class
of the reflexive-transitive closure of $\triangle$.
A subgraph is \trcon{} component if it is a maximal \trcon{} subgraph.
Since every 3-cycle has to be monochromatic in all NAC-colorings,
every \trcon{} component has to be monochromatic as well,
which gives a naive algorithm to compute all NAC-colorings used in \flexrilog.
In the second part of this paper, we focus on approaches that significantly improve this algorithm.
Our implementation is faster than \flexrilog{} by two orders of magnitude.

The complexity results on the existence of a NAC-coloring in sparse graphs are presented
in \Cref{sec:NP} as well as showing fixed parameter tractability when parametrized by treewidth.
In \Cref{sec:alg} we propose various approaches how to compute NAC-colorings
faster than using the naive algorithm and we compare them
by presenting benchmarks in \Cref{sec:benchmarks}.


\section{NP-completeness on sparse graphs}
\label{sec:NP}

In this section we prove that the question
whether a NAC-coloring exists is NP-complete on the class of graphs with maximum
degree five and also on the class of graphs with average degree smaller
than $4+\varepsilon$ of any $\varepsilon >0$.
We proceed by reduction from the 3-SAT problem,
namely, the question if a boolean formula
in conjunctive normal form in which each clause contains exactly three literals is satisfiable.
The main idea of our proof is inspired by
the proof of NAC-coloring NP-completeness~\cite{np_complete}.
We propose a different gadget construction that allows us to limit the maximum
degree in the constructed graph.

\begin{theorem}
	\label{theorem:nac-deg-5}
	%
	The question whether a graph has a NAC-coloring is NP-complete
	on the class of graphs with maximum degree five.
	%
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
	%
	Let $\phi$ be a formula with variables $x_{1}, \dots, x_{n}$
	and clauses $L_1, \dots, L_k$.
	Our goal is to construct a graph $G_\phi$
	of size $O(n+k)$ such that $\phi$ is satisfiable if and only if
	$G_\phi$ has a NAC-coloring.

	We exploit the fact that \trcon{} components
	are monochromatic in every NAC-coloring.
	In particular, every subgraph isomorphic to a ladder graph with diagonals is monochromatic.
	We call a ladder graph such that every 4-cycle has one diagonal a \emph{braced ladder}.
	We build a 2-tree structure called a \emph{train},
	which is a ``horizontal'' braced ladder with other ``vertical'' braced ladders
	glued so that the maximum degree is 5, see \Cref{fig:proof_trains}.
	A train can be extended arbitrarily to connect more braced ladders.

