\section{Conclusion}

It is well known that minimally rigid graphs $G$ satisfy $|E(G)|=2|V(G)|-3$.
There is a paper in preparation containing a proof of the conjecture from~\cite{GLS2019}:
a minimally rigid graph $G$ has a NAC-coloring if and only if
$G$ is not a 2-tree.
The latter condition can be checked in polynomial time.
Hence, for graphs with $|E(G)|=2|V(G)|-3$ it can be decided
in polynomial time whether they have a NAC-coloring, since if they are not minimally rigid,
which can be checked in polynomial time by \cite{polynomial-min-rigid},
they are flexible and so they have a NAC-coloring.
Since we have shown that the NAC-coloring existence is NP-complete for graphs
with $|E(G)|\leq (2+\varepsilon)|V(G)|$,
the only cases for which the complexity question remains open is when
$|E(G)|\leq 2|V(G)|+c$, where $c\geq -2$ is a fixed constant.
In particular, it is open whether the existence of a NAC-coloring is NP-complete
on graphs of maximum degree 4.
Regarding fixed parameter tractability, Courcelle's theorem does not provide
a good bound on the dependence on the tree width.
Hence, we would like to design an explicit algorithm using tree decomposition.
Obviously, one could consider other graph parameters.

The algorithms we have proposed together with their implementation outperform
the naive one in \flexrilog{} by two orders of magnitude.
We aim to include our code directly in the package PyRigi~\cite{pyrigi}.
In this paper, we have described only the heuristics that were performing well,
while we have tried many others.
It may be beneficial to rewrite promising algorithms
in a static compiled low level language like C, C++ or Zig.

We have not employed the following optimization to speed up
the \IsNACColoring{} check: here we iterate
bit-masks encoding red-blue colorings linearly,
while one could instead iterate using Gray code,
which guarantees that only one monochromatic class changes its color.
Exploiting this could save some computational time.

The described approaches are suitable for extending to special kinds of
NAC-colorings like \emph{cartesian}
or \emph{rotationally symmetric} NAC-colorings, see for instance~\cite{GL2024}
for the definitions.

