\begin{abstract}
Scientific applications typically generate large volumes of floating-point data, making lossy compression one of the most effective methods for data reduction, thereby lowering storage requirements and improving performance in large-scale applications.
% By leveraging prediction or transformation techniques, it reduces information density, enabling substantial reductions in data volume. 
% Previous research on the performance of lossy compression has predominantly focused on compression ratio, while the prediction of compression time has received comparatively less attention. 
However, variations in compression time can significantly impact overall performance improvement, due to inaccurate scheduling, workload imbalances, etc.
Existing approaches rely on empirical methods to predict the compression performance, which often lack interpretability and suffer from limitations in accuracy and generalizability. 
In this paper, we propose surrogate models for predicting the compression time of prediction-based lossy compression and provide a detailed analysis of the factors influencing time variability with uncertainty analysis. 
