\section{Preliminary results}

Initial tests didn't produce satisfactory results. Only by refining the prompt structure and wording the models started to return consistent and well formatted output. For example the first results return very few rows, with very few details and incomplete examples and empty cells or duplicate rows. However, when a refined prompt were used, the number of rows tend to match or exceed the manual taxonomy, as well as the level of details and examples without introducing the manual constrains, i.e: ask for a number of rows. 

There was a clear quality improve produced by the introduction of the text of the documentation inside the user prompt. However this methodology is constrained by the context length of the model measured in tokens. This is not a limitation given that the context for modern LLMs ranges from 32K to 128K tokens, while the most extensive Qiskit documentation is about 20K tokens. See Fig. \ref{fig:qiskit_versions}. The mentioned context length limitation can be walk around by the uses techniques such as RAG or context summarization.

% Regarding the evolution of the experiment and the results obtained, we can comment that initially they were not satisfactory due to being insufficiently comprehensive, presenting replication and clustering of scenarios. These difficulties were successfully mitigated through prompt engineering, refining aspects related to the semantics of the desired results, the use of constraints and their structuring, as well as exemplifications. A limiting scenario we faced was the incorporation of external textual information from official sources, and the issue of the number of tokens supported by the context of the target model.

