\subsection{The \texorpdfstring{$\Av$-$\Phi$}{A-Phi} Formulation}
Assuming a harmonic time dependence of the form $\exp(-i\omega t)$, Maxwell's equations are given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:EH_eqs}
 \begin{aligned}
         &\Curl \Ev = i\omega \Bv, \\
     &\Curl \Hv = -i\omega \Dv+ \Jv,  \\   
     &\nabla \cdot \Bv = 0,  \\
     & \nabla \cdot \Dv = \rho,
    \end{aligned}   
\end{equation}
where $\omega$ is the angular frequency, $\Ev$ and $\Hv$ are the electric and magnetic fields, and $\Dv$ and $\Bv$ are electric and magnetic flux densities. The sources are given by the current density $\Jv$ and the charge density $\rho$. In addition to Eq.~\eqref{eq:EH_eqs}, we also have the constitutive relations $ \Dv = \vare_0 \vare(\rs) \Ev$ and $\Bv = \mu_0 \mu(\rs)\Hv$, and the charge continuity equation $\nabla \cdot \Jv = i\omega \rho$, where $\vare_0$ and $\mu_0$ are the free-space permittivity and permeability, respectively.

The magnetic vector potential $\Av$ and the electric scalar potential $\Phi$, satisfy~\cite{jackson1998} 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:1}
 \Bv =  \Curl \Av,   
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:2}
   \quad \Ev = i\omega \Av - \nabla \Phi.
\end{equation}
Substituting Eqs.~\eqref{eq:1} and~\eqref{eq:2} into Eq.~\eqref{eq:EH_eqs}, it can be shown that the potentials satisfy~\cite{harrington61}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:3}
 \Curl \Curl \Av - \omega^2 \mu_0 \vare_0 \vare(\rs) \Av  - i \omega \mu_0\vare_0 \vare (\rs)\nabla \Phi  = \mu_0 \Jv,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:4}
 \nabla \cdot \brc{\vare(\rs)\nabla \Phi}  - i \nabla \cdot \brc{\omega \vare(\rs) \Av}    =  -\rho/\vare_0.
\end{equation}
Let $\Ac = \tilde{A}_x \hat{\boldsymbol{x}}+\tilde{A}_y \hat{\boldsymbol{y}}+\tilde{A}_z \hat{\boldsymbol{z}} := \omega\Av$, then Eqs.~\eqref{eq:3} and~\eqref{eq:4} can be rewritten as 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:A}
 \Curl \Curl \Ac - k_0^2 \vare(\rs) \Ac  - i k_0^2 \vare (\rs)\nabla \Phi  = k_0\eta_0 \Jv,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:4n}
 \nabla \cdot \brc{\vare(\rs)\nabla \Phi}  - i \nabla \cdot \brc{\vare(\rs)\Ac}    =  \frac{i\eta_0}{k_0}\nabla \cdot \Jv,
\end{equation}
where $k_0 = \omega \sqrt{\mu_0 \vare_0}$ is the free-space wavenumber, and $\eta_0 = \sqrt{\mu_0/\vare_0}$ is the free-space characteristic impedance. Notice that the charge continuity equation has been used in Eq.~\eqref{eq:4n}. By using the identity $\Curl\Curl \Ac = \nabla^2 \Ac - \nabla(\Div\Ac)$ and substituting in Eq.~\eqref{eq:A}, we obtain
\begin{equation}\label{eq:newA}
  \nabla^2 \Ac + k_0^2 \vare(\rs) \Ac  - \nabla(\Div\Ac) + i k_0^2 \vare (\rs)\nabla \Phi = - k_0\eta_0 \Jv,   
\end{equation}
By introducing the Lorenz gauge $\Div \Ac = i k_0^2\vare(\rs)\Phi$ into Eqs.~\eqref{eq:4n}~and~\eqref{eq:newA}, we obtain the following system of differential equations:
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:Contin}
   \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2} % Adjust row spacing
    \setlength{\arraycolsep}{6pt} % Adjust column spacing
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \mathcal{V}              & 0  & 0 & -i\partial_x \vare(\rs)\\
         0  & \mathcal{V}  &  0 & -i\partial_y \vare(\rs) \\
         0  &  0  & \mathcal{V}  &  -i\partial_z \vare(\rs)\\
        -ik_0^2\partial_x \vare(\rs) &  -ik_0^2\partial_y \vare(\rs)  & -ik_0^2\partial_z \vare(\rs)& \mathcal{L} \\
    \end{bmatrix}  \begin{bmatrix}
        \tilde{A}_x\\
       \tilde{A}_y\\
      \tilde{A}_z\\
      {\Phi}_s
    \end{bmatrix} = -\eta_0 k_0\begin{bmatrix}
        J_x\\
         J_y\\
        J_z\\
        \frac{\Div \Jv}{i}
    \end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}\label{eq:OPV}
\mathcal{V} \tilde{A}_\tau := \nabla^2 \tilde{A}_\tau + k_0^2 \vare(r) \tilde{A}_\tau, \quad 
\end{equation}
for each $\tau \in\set{x,y,z}$, 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:OPL}
\mathcal{L} {\Phi}_s  := \Div(\vare(\rs)\nabla {\Phi}_s ) + k_0^2\vare^2(\rs){\Phi}_s,
\end{equation}
and ${\Phi}_s := k_0^2 \Phi$.
\subsection{Decoupled $\Av$-$\Phi$ SIEs}\label{sec2_2}
In $\Omega_1$, where the medium is homogeneous with $\vare(\rs) = 1$ (see Fig.~\ref{Figure_1}), the equations comprising system~\eqref{eq:Contin} decouple. Assuming there exist \textit{impressed} free current $\Jv$ and charge $\rho$, the incident potentials are given by
\[
 \inc{\tilde{\mathbf{A}}}(\rs) = k_0\eta_0\sL[\Jv](\rs), \quad   \inc{\Phi}(\rs) =\frac{1}{\vare_0\vare(\rs)}\sL[\rho](\rs).
\]
For the case of an incident plane wave with a polarization vector $\boldsymbol{E}_p$ and a propagation direction $\hat{\boldsymbol{u}}$, given by
 \[
 \inc{\mathbf{E}}(\rs)  = \boldsymbol{E}_p e^{i k_0\hat{\boldsymbol{u}} \cdot \rs},\quad  \inc{\mathbf{H}}(\rs)  = \frac{\hat{\boldsymbol{u}}\times \boldsymbol{E}_p}{\eta_0} e^{i k_0\hat{\boldsymbol{u}}\cdot \rs},
 \]
the incident fields $\inc{\Phi}$ and $\inc{\tilde{A}}_\tau$ can be found by decomposing $\inc{\mathbf{E}}(\rs)$ and $ \inc{\mathbf{H}}(\rs) $ into 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:incAPhi}
    \begin{aligned}
\inc{\Phi}(\rs) &= -(\rs \cdot \boldsymbol{E}_p) \exp(i k_0\hat{\boldsymbol{u}}\cdot \rs), \\
\inc{\tilde{\mathbf{A}}}(\rs) &= -k_0(\rs \cdot \boldsymbol{E}_p)  \exp(i k_0\hat{\boldsymbol{u}}\cdot \rs)\,\hat{\boldsymbol{u}}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
This decomposition, originally proposed by Vico \textit{et al.}~\cite{vico2016}, satisfies the identity 
$\mathbf{E}^{\mathrm{inc}} = - \nabla \inc{\Phi} + i \tilde{\mathbf{A}}^{(\mathrm{inc})}$ 
along with the Lorenz gauge condition 
$\Div \inc{\tilde{\mathbf{A}}}= i k_0^2 \vare(\rs)\,\inc{\Phi}$. 
Moreover, it ensures that both $\inc{\tilde{\mathbf{A}}}$ and $\inc{\Phi}$ 
remain bounded as $k_0 \to 0$. 

With the definition of the incident potentials $\set{\tilde{A}_x^{(\mathrm{inc})}, \tilde{A}_y^{(\mathrm{inc})} , \tilde{A}_z^{(\mathrm{inc})}, \Phi^{(\mathrm{inc})}}$, the integral equations for $\tilde{A}_x, \tilde{A}_y, \tilde{A}_z$ and $\Phi$ are given by Eqs.~\eqref{eq:13} and~\eqref{eq:14}. By taking the limit of Eqs.~\eqref{eq:13} and~\eqref{eq:14} as $\rs \to \rs_0 \in \GA$, and imposing the jump conditions~\cite{colton_kress98}
\begin{equation}\label{Eq::JUMP}
    \begin{aligned}
 &\lim\limits_{\substack{\delta \to 0\\\delta >0}} \brc{\sL\psi}(\rs_0 + \delta \nn(\rs_0)) = \brc{\sL\psi}(\rs_0), \\
 &  \lim\limits_{\substack{\delta \to 0\\\delta >0}} \brc{\dL\psi}(\rs_0 + \delta \nn(\rs_0)) =  \brc{\widetilde{\dL}\psi}(\rs_0)+ \frac{1}{2}\psi(\rs_0),
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
we obtain the following SIE for each $\alpha \in \set{\tilde{A}_x, \tilde{A}_y, \tilde{A}_z, \Phi}$ and $\beta = \partial \alpha/\partial\nn$:
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:SIE_Phi_A}
      \frac{\alpha(\rs) }{2}- \brc{\widetilde{\dL}\alpha}(\rs) + \brc{ \sL \beta}(\rs)   =  \alpha^{(\mathrm{inc})}(\rs),\quad  \rs \in \GA
\end{equation}
 where the integral $\widetilde{\dL}$ is a Cauchy principal value. As clear from Eq.~\eqref{eq:SIE_Phi_A}, only the single-layer $\mathcal{S}\beta$ and double-layer $\widetilde{\dL}\alpha$ potentials need to be approximated at the surface $\Gamma$. Furthermore, since the surface $\Gamma$, by construction, encloses the inhomogeneous region and is entirely embedded in free space, the boundary conditions for the electric scalar potential and each component of the vector potential are naturally continuous across $\Gamma$. Specifically, we impose the continuity of the fields and their normal derivatives as
 \begin{equation}\label{eq:BCs}
  \alpha|_{\Gamma^-} = \alpha|_{\Gamma^+},\quad \beta|_{\Gamma^-}= \beta|_{\Gamma^+}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{$\Av$-$\Phi$ formulation in exterior calculus notation}
Exterior calculus provides an elegant framework for expressing Maxwell's equations through differential $k$-forms, which are mathematical objects naturally suited for integration over $k$-dimensional domains~\cite{deschamps1981,flanders1989,warnick1997,frankel2011}. For example, the line integral $\int E_x \ed x + E_y \ed y + E_z \ed z$, the surface integral $\int B_x \ed y \wedge \ed z + B_y \ed z \wedge \ed x + B_z \ed x \wedge \ed y$, and the volume integral $\int \rho(x,y,z) \ed x \wedge \ed y \wedge \ed z$ correspond to the 1-form $\Ef$, the 2-form $\Bf$, and the 3-form $\boldsymbol{\rho}$ defined as
\begin{equation*}
    \begin{aligned}
        \Ef &:= E_x \ed x + E_y \ed y + E_z \ed z, \\
       \Bf &:= B_x \ed y \wedge \ed z + B_y \ed z \wedge \ed x + B_z \ed x \wedge \ed y, \\
    \boldsymbol{\rho} &:= \rho \ed x \wedge \ed y \wedge \ed z .   
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
In this notation, $\ed$ represents the exterior derivative operator that maps a $k$-form $\boldsymbol{\alpha}$ to a $(k+1)$-form $\ed\boldsymbol{\alpha}$ in accordance with the generalized Stokes's formula
\begin{equation}\label{eq:stokes}
  \int_{\partial \mathcal{M}} \boldsymbol{\alpha} = \int_{\mathcal{M}} \ed \boldsymbol{\alpha},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{M}$ is a $(k+1)$-dimensional oriented manifold with boundary $\partial \mathcal{M}$. The degree $k$ of a differential $k$-form determines its integration domain, a $k$-dimensional manifold. For a manifold $\mathcal{M} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$, the maximum degree of forms is limited by $k \leq n$. The wedge product $\wedge$ combines forms and defines the basis for $k$-vector spaces in exterior algebra~\cite{flanders1989}. These oriented integrals follow the algebraic rules $\ed x \wedge \ed x = 0$ and $\ed x \wedge \ed y = -\ed y \wedge \ed x$.

Another crucial operator is the Hodge star operator $\star$, and it complements $\ed$ and $\wedge$ in this formalism. For $k = 0, \ldots, n$, if $\bigwedge^k(\mathcal{M})$ represents the vector space of differential $k$-forms on $\mathcal{M} \subset \mathbb{R}^n$~\cite{flanders1989}, then the Hodge star maps $k$-forms to $(n-k)$-forms:
\[ \star: \bigwedge\nolimits^{k}(\mathcal{M}) \longrightarrow \bigwedge\nolimits^{n-k}(\mathcal{M}). \]
To represent Maxwell’s equations and the constitutive relations in the language of differential forms, we also utilize the flat $\flat$ and sharp $\sharp$ operators, which provide a natural isomorphism between vector fields and $1$-forms (and vice
versa). Specifically, for vector fields $\Ev$, $\Hv$, and $\Av$, we have their corresponding 1-forms $\boldsymbol{E} = \Ev^\flat$, $\boldsymbol{H} = \Hv^\flat$, and $\boldsymbol{A} = \Av^\flat$. In addition, these operators allow us to express vector calculus operations in terms of exterior derivatives and the Hodge star. For
a vector field $\Av$ in $\mathbb{R}^3$ and a scalar field $\Phi$, the following relations hold~\cite{mohammed2016}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ECI}
\begin{aligned}
(\Curl \Av)^\flat &= \star\ed\Av^\flat, \\
(\Div \Av)^\flat &= \star\ed\star\Av^\flat,\\
(\nabla \Phi)^\flat & = \ed \Phi.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
By applying the $\flat$ operator to Maxwell's equations~\eqref{eq:EH_eqs}, followed by an application of the Hodge star operator and using the identities~\eqref{eq:ECI}, we obtain the differential forms representation of Maxwell's equations:
\begin{equation*}
\ed \Ef = i\omega \Bf,\quad \ed \Hf = -i\omega \Df + \Jf, \quad \ed \Bf = 0, \quad \ed \Df = \tilde{\rho},
\end{equation*}
where the identity $\star \star \boldsymbol{\alpha} = \boldsymbol{\alpha}$, for any $k$-form $\boldsymbol{\alpha}\in \bigwedge^{k}(\mathbb{R}^3)$, has been used. For the constitutive relations 
\[
\Dv = \vare_0 \vare(\rs) \Ev,\quad \Bv = \mu_0 \mu(\rs) \Hv,\]
the identities $\Df = \star \Dv^\flat$ and $\Bf = \star \Bv^\flat$ lead to
\[ \Df = \vare_0\star_{\vare}\Ef,\quad \Bf = \mu_0 \star_{\mu} \Hf. \]
Here $\star_\vare$ and $\star_\mu$ emphasize the dependence of the Hodge star operators on the underlying metric. Their discrete constructions are detailed in Section~\ref{sec3.4}.

This differential forms representation naturally aligns with the fundamental laws of electromagnetism. For instance, Faraday's law states that $\oint_{\partial S} \Ev \cdot d\mathbf{l} + \partial_t \int_S\Bv \cdot d\mathbf{S} = 0$, relating the circulation of the electric field along a closed loop $\partial S$ to the time rate of change of the magnetic flux density through surface $S$. This physical relationship is elegantly captured by representing $\Ef$ as a 1-form and $\Bf$ as a 2-form. For readers unfamiliar with exterior calculus, references~\cite{boyuan2023,perot2014} provide a concise introduction. 

In this exterior calculus (EC) notation, the system of equations~\eqref{eq:Contin} takes the form
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:Contin_DEC}
   \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2} % Adjust row spacing
    \setlength{\arraycolsep}{6pt} % Adjust column spacing
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{EC}}              & 0  & 0 & -i\star_{\partial_x \vare(\rs)}\\
         0  & \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{EC}}  &  0 & -i\star_{\partial_y \vare(\rs)} \\
         0  &  0  & \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{EC}}  &  -i\star_{\partial_z \vare(\rs)}\\
        -ik_0^2\star_{\partial_x \vare(\rs)} &  -ik_0^2\star_{\partial_y \vare(\rs)}  & -ik_0^2\star_{\partial_z \vare(\rs)}& \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{EC}} \\
    \end{bmatrix}  \begin{bmatrix}
        \tilde{A}_x\\
       \tilde{A}_y\\
      \tilde{A}_z\\
       {\Phi}_s
    \end{bmatrix} = -\eta_0 k_0 \star \begin{bmatrix}
        J_x\\
         J_y\\
        J_z\\
        \frac{\Div \Jv}{i}
    \end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}\label{eq:OPV_EC}
\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{EC}}  :=  \ed \star \ed + k_0^2 \star_{\vare} ,
\end{equation}
 and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:OPL_EC}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{EC}}    := \ed \star_{\vare} \ed + k_0^2 \star_{\vare^2}.
\end{equation}
Notice that the 0-forms $\tilde{A}_x,\,\tilde{A}_y,\,\tilde{A}_z$, and $ {\Phi}_s$ are simply scalar fields in the exterior calculus notation.  
