\section{The DEC framework}\label{sec3}
In this section, the discrete exterior calculus (DEC) framework is briefly introduced to solve the system~\eqref{eq:Contin_DEC} on the bounded simulation domain $\Omega_2 = \Omega_a \cup\Omega_s$ (see Fig.~\ref{Figure_1}). The notation of the simplicial complex, which provides a discrete approximation to the $\Omega_2$ is introduced first. This is followed by the description of discrete differential forms (also called cochains, or degrees-of-freedom arrays (DoF-arrays)~\cite{Bossavit1999}), defined on the simplicial complex and its dual. The discrete exterior derivative and Hodge star operators are then briefly discussed. Such operators act on the discrete differential forms and make it possible to write the operators in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:Contin_DEC}-\eqref{eq:OPL} as matrices. For more details on the definitions of simplicial and cell complexes and discrete exterior calculus operators, the readers may refer to~\cite{hirani2003discrete,desbrun2005discrete}.
%%=========================== Section 3.1 ===================================
 \subsection{The simplicial complex}\label{sec3.1}
Let $\mathcal{K}$ be the simplicial complex approximating the bounded domain $\Omega_2$ (see Fig.~\ref{Figure_1}). The complex $\mathcal{K}$ is a collection of $k$-simplices. For $k = 0,\ldots,3$, a $k$-simplex $\sigma^k\in \mathcal{K}$ is defined by the nodes forming it as $\sigma^k = [v_1,\ldots, v_{k+1}]$, where the subscripts designate the nodes indices~\cite{hirani2003discrete}. The set of 0-simplices contains the nodes $\set{v_1, \ldots, v_{N_0}}$, where $N_0$ is the number of nodes. The set of 1-simplices contains the edges $\set{e_1, \ldots, e_{N_1}}$, where $e_k\equiv \sigma^1_k$ is an edge defined by the nodes $v_{k_1}$ and $v_{k_2}$ forming it as $e_k = [v_{k_1}, v_{k_{2}}]$, and $N_1$ denotes the number of edges. The orientation of the edge is implied by the order of its nodes. Similarly, the set of 2-simplices contains all the triangles $\set{t_1,\ldots, t_{N_2}}$, where $t_k\equiv \sigma^2_k$ is a triangle defined as $t_k = [v_{k_1}, v_{k_2}, v_{k_3}]$, and $N_2$ is the number of triangles. Finally, the 3-simplices contains the number of $N_3$ tetrahedra $\set{ \mathcal{T}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{T}_{N_3}} $, where $\mathcal{T}_k\equiv \sigma^3_k$ is a tetrahedron defined as $\mathcal{T}_k = [v_{k_1}, v_{k_2}, v_{k_3}, v_{k_4}]$. The highest-dimensional 3-simplices are assumed to have the same orientation, which is typically ensured by the mesh generation tool. The orientation of $k$-simplices with $k<3$ is arbitrary. Here we assume the edges are oriented such that any edge $e_k = [v_{k_1}, v_{k_2}]$ is oriented such that the indices $k_1 < k_2$. Similarly, we assume any triangle $t_k = [v_{k_1}, v_{k_2}, v_{k_3}]$ is oriented such that the indices $k_1 < k_2 < k_3$. 

In addition to the primal simplicial complex, one can also construct its dual, denoted by $\ast\mathcal{K}$, consisting of cells. The circumcentric or barycentric dual complexes can be used to construct discrete approximations to the Hodge star operators~\cite{hirani2003discrete,Kurz}. The choice of the dual mesh affects both the accuracy and structure of the resulting discrete Hodge star matrices. For example, choosing the circumcentric dual mesh leads to diagonal Hodge star matrices~\cite{hirani2003discrete}. It is not always ensured that all circumcenters are located inside their tetrahedra. Hence, Hodge-optimized triangulations have been shown to solve this problem by promoting the use of power diagrams to create a weighted dual mesh~\cite{mullen2011hot}. Another discrete definitions of the Hodge star are based on Whitney forms~\cite{whiney1957}. They either require the barycentric dual mesh to construct a geometrical barycentric-based Hodge matrices~\cite{Kurz} or use the Galerkin approach that does not require the dual mesh for its definition~\cite{Bossavit1999}. The Hodge star approximations based on Whitney forms are sparse but non-diagonal matrices. In this paper, we use the Galerkin Hodge star definitions. 
%%=========================== Section 3.2 ===================================
\subsection{Discrete differential forms}
Since the simulation domain is now approximated by a simplicial complex, the smooth differential forms are also discretized. By discretization of a smooth differential $k$-form over a complex, we think of its integration over all $k$-dimensional mesh objects. In the smooth case, there is no distinction made between the 1-forms $\Ef$ and $\Hf$, and the 2-forms $\Df$ and $\Bf$. However, for discrete differential forms, a distinction between the nature of these forms must be clearly made~\cite{teixeira1999lattice}. The forms $\Ef$ and $\Bf$ have internal orientations, so they are known as ordinary forms. The forms $\Hf$ and $\Df$ have external orientations and are referred to as twisted forms. It is then convenient to discretize each kind on a distinct mesh. Ordinary forms can be associated with a primal simplicial complex $\mathcal{K}$, while the twisted forms can be associated with a dual cell complex $\ast\mathcal{K}$. The resulting spaces of discrete differential $k$-forms are denoted by $\mathcal{C}^k(\mathcal{K})$ for discrete forms defined on the primal mesh complex, and are denoted by $\mathfrak{D}^k(\ast\mathcal{K})$ for discrete forms defined on the dual mesh complex. Discretization of the smooth differential forms on the primal and dual complexes is performed via the de Rham map. The maps that discretize the primal zero form $\pf$, 1-form $\Ef$ and 2-form $\Bf$ to yield the DoF-arrays $\overline{\mathbf{\Phi}}$, $\overline{\mathbf{e}}$ and $\overline{\mathbf{b}}$, respectively, are defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:deRhamMapsPrime}
    \begin{aligned}
\mathcal{R}_{\pf}: {\bigwedge}^0 (\mathcal{M})  \longrightarrow {\mathcal{C}}^0(\mathcal{K}),\quad  \pf \longmapsto \overline{\mathbf{\Phi}} = [\Phi(v_1),\ldots, \Phi(v_{N_0})]^\mathsf{T}\\
\mathcal{R}_{\Ef}: {\bigwedge}^1 (\mathcal{M})  \longrightarrow {\mathcal{C}}^1(\mathcal{K}), \quad  \Ef \longmapsto  \overline{\mathbf{e}} =   \brc{ \int_{e_1}\Ef,\ldots,\int_{e_{N_1}} \Ef}^\mathsf{T},\\
\mathcal{R}_{\Bf}: {\bigwedge}^2 (\mathcal{M})  \longrightarrow {\mathcal{C}}^2(\mathcal{K}), \quad  \Bf \longmapsto  \overline{\mathbf{b}} =   \brc{ \int_{t_1}\Bf,\ldots,\int_{t_{N_2}} \Bf}^\mathsf{T},
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where the superscript $\mathsf{T}$ denotes the transpose. Although our exterior calculus formulation in~\eqref{eq:Contin_DEC} involves only 0-forms, it is essential to understand how discrete differential forms of various degrees are defined. This is because higher-order forms inevitably appear in the discretization process. For example, the exterior derivative \(\ed \Phi\) is a 1-form and is discretized as values on primal edges, while the Hodge dual \(\star \Phi\) is a 3-form, represented by scalar values associated with dual volumes, which correspond to the primal nodes.
\subsection{Discrete exterior derivatives}\label{sec3.3}
The discrete exterior derivative operator denoted by $\ded_k$ maps primal $k$-forms to primal $(k+1)$-forms. Consider the 1-form \(\ed\Phi\), which is discretized over the primal edges \(e_k\). By applying Stokes's theorem~\eqref{eq:stokes}, the \(k\)th entry of the degree-of-freedom (DoF) array obtained via the de~Rham map, denoted by \([\mathcal{R}_{\ed\Phi}]_k\), is given by 

\[
[\mathcal{R}_{\ed\pf}]_k = \int_{e_k} \ed\pf \overset{Stokes}{=} \int_{\partial e_k} \pf =  \Phi(v_{k_2}) -  \Phi(v_{k_1}) = \sum_\ell [\ded_0]_{k,\ell} \Phi_\ell, \quad k = 1,\ldots,N_1. 
\]
where $\Phi_\ell = \Phi(v_\ell)$. Hence the discrete exterior derivative $\ded_0$ is an $N_1\times N_0$ matrix defined as
\[
\brc{\ded_0}_{k\ell} = \begin{cases}
    +1, & \text{if the node $\ell$ is the end node of the edge $k$}, \\
    -1, & \text{if the node $\ell$ is the start node of the edge $k$}, \\
    0,  &  \text{if the node $\ell$ is not a face of the edge $k$},
\end{cases}
\]
that maps the discrete 0-form $\overline{\mathbf{\Phi}}$ to the discrete 1-form $\ded_0 \overline{\mathbf{\Phi}}$ as
\[
\overline{\mathbf{\Phi}} \longmapsto \ded_0 \overline{\mathbf{\Phi}} = \brc{ \int_{e_1}\ed\Phi,\ldots,\int_{e_{N_1}} \ed\Phi }^\mathsf{T}.
\]
Similar definitions follow for $\ded_1$ and $\ded_2$. Consider the discretization of the 2-form $\ed\Ef$ over the triangle $t_k$, we have 
\[
[\mathcal{R}_{\ed\Ef}]_k = \int_{t_k} \ed\Ef  \overset{Stokes}{=} \oint_{\partial t_k} \Phi =  \sum_\ell [\ded_1]_{k,\ell} \overline{\mathbf{e}}_\ell, \quad k = 1,\ldots,N_2.  
\]
where $\overline{\mathbf{e}}_\ell$ is the $l$th entry of the array $\overline{\mathbf{e}}$. The discrete exterior derivative $\ded_1$ is an $N_2\times N_1$ matrix defined as
\[
\brc{\ded_1}_{k\ell} = \begin{cases}
    +1, & \text{if the edge $\ell$ is a face of the triangle $k$, with consistent orientation}, \\
    -1, & \text{if the edge $\ell$ is a face of the triangle $k$, with opposite orientation}, \\
    0,  &  \text{if the edge $\ell$ is not a face of the triangle $k$},
\end{cases}
\]
that maps the discrete 1-form $\overline{\mathbf{e}}$ to the discrete 2-form $\ded_1 \overline{\mathbf{e}}$ as
\[
\overline{\mathbf{e}} \longmapsto \ded_1 \overline{\mathbf{e}} = \brc{ \int_{t_1}\ed\Ef,\ldots,\int_{t_{N_2}} \ed\Ef }^\mathsf{T}.
\]
Finally, the discrete exterior derivative $\ded_2$ is an $N_3\times N_2$ matrix defined as
\[
\brc{\ded_2}_{k\ell} = \begin{cases}
    +1, & \text{if the triangle $\ell$ is a face of the tetrahedron $k$, with consistent orientation}, \\
    -1, & \text{if the triangle $\ell$ is a face of the tetrahedron $k$, with opposite orientation}, \\
    0,  &  \text{if the triangle $\ell$ is not a face of the tetrahedron  $k$},
\end{cases}
\]
that maps the discrete 2-form $\overline{\mathbf{b}}$ to the discrete 3-form $\ded_2 \overline{\mathbf{b}}$ as 
\[
\overline{\mathbf{b}} \longmapsto \ded_2 \overline{\mathbf{b}} = \brc{ \int_{\mathcal{T}_1}\ed\Bf,\ldots,\int_{\mathcal{T}_{N_3}} \ed\Bf }^\mathsf{T}.
\]

The orientation of the primal mesh $k$-simplices induces their dual orientation. The discrete exterior derivative operator $\ded_k^\ast$ maps dual $k$-forms to dual (\(k+1\))-forms and are given by~\cite{desbrun2005discrete}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:d_duals}
 \ded_k^\ast = (-1)^{(n-k)}\, \ded_{(n-k-1)}^\mathsf{T}.
\end{equation}
For $n = 3$, using Eq.~\eqref{eq:d_duals} gives
\[
 \ded_0^\ast = - \ded_{2}^\mathsf{T}, \quad \ded_1^\ast =   \ded_{1}^\mathsf{T},\quad \ded_2^\ast = - \ded_{0}^\mathsf{T}.
\]
\subsection{The Galerkin Hodge star}\label{sec3.4}
The discrete Hodge star operators we use are the Galerkin Hodge star operators denoted by $\hd_k$. The operator $\hd_k$ maps discrete differential $k$-forms to $(n-k)$-forms. For the constitutive relations $\Df = \star_\vare \Ef$ and $\Hf = \star_{\mu^{-1}}\Bf$, we designate the material parameters dependence as
\[
\overline{\mathbf{d}} = \hd_1(\vare) \overline{\mathbf{e}}, \quad \overline{\mathbf{h}} = \hd_2(\mu^{-1}) \overline{\mathbf{b}},
\]
where $\hd_k(\cdot)$ is an $N_k\times N_k$ matrix, $k = 0,\ldots,3$. The construction of the matrices $\hd_k$ involves the use of Whitney forms $\Wf{k}$ or their proxy fields obtained via the sharp $\sharp$ operator that maps 1-forms to vector fields. For details on Whitney forms and their proxy fields, the reader may refer to~\cite{whiney1957,lohi2021}. 

Given a tetrahedron $\mathcal{T}$ with nodes denoted locally as $[v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4]$, a Whitney 0-form $\Wf{0}_\ell$ is associated with each node $v_\ell,\, \ell = 1,\ldots, 4$, and is given by
\[
\Wf{0}_{v_\ell} = \lambda_\ell(\rs),
\]
where $\lambda(\rs)$ is the barycentric coordinates for the tetrahedron $\mathcal{T}$. Since 0-forms are scalars, there is no distinction between the Whitney 0-forms as their proxy fields. The Whitney 1-forms are associated with edges. For a tetrahedron $\mathcal{T}$, we have the locally-indexed edges $e_\ell, \,\ell = 1,\ldots,6$, where the edge $e_\ell$ is defined by the nodes forming it as $e_l = [v_{l_1},v_{l_2}]$. The Whitney 1-form associated with the edge $e_\ell$ is
\[
\Wf{1}_{e_\ell} = \lambda_{l_1}(\rs)\ed \lambda_{l_2}(\rs)-  \lambda_{l_2}(\rs)\ed \lambda_{l_1}(\rs),
\]
while its proxy field $\Wv{1}_{e_\ell}$ is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Whitney1Proxy}
    \Wv{1}_{e_\ell} = \lambda_{l_1}(\rs)\nabla \lambda_{l_2}(\rs)-  \lambda_{l_2}(\rs)\nabla \lambda_{l_1}(\rs). 
\end{equation}
The Whitney 2-forms are associated with the triangles of $\mathcal{T}$. The triangle of $\mathcal{T}$ are $t_\ell,\, \ell = 1,\ldots,4$. As before, the triangle $t_\ell$ is defined by the nodes forming it as $t_l = [v_{l_1},v_{l_2},v_{l_3}]$ and the Whitney 2-form associated with this triangle is
\[
\Wf{2}_{t_\ell} = 2\brc{\lambda_{l_1}(\rs)\ed \lambda_{l_2}(\rs)\wedge\ed\lambda_{l_3}(\rs) +   \lambda_{l_2}(\rs)\ed \lambda_{l_3}(\rs)\wedge\ed\lambda_{l_1}(\rs) +\lambda_{l_3}(\rs)\ed \lambda_{l_1}(\rs)\wedge\ed\lambda_{l_2}(\rs)}.
\]
The proxy field $\Wv{2}_{t_\ell}$ of the 2-form $\Wf{2}_{t_\ell}$ can be written as $(\star \Wf{2})^\sharp$, and is given by~\cite{lohi2021}
\[
\Wv{2}_{t_\ell} = 2\brc{\lambda_{l_1}(\rs)\nabla \lambda_{l_2}(\rs)\times\nabla\lambda_{l_3}(\rs) +\lambda_{l_2}(\rs)\nabla \lambda_{l_3}(\rs)\times \nabla \lambda_{l_1}(\rs) +\lambda_{l_3}(\rs)\nabla  \lambda_{l_1}(\rs)\times\nabla \lambda_{l_2}(\rs)}.
\]
Given any bounded function $\xi(\rs)$ that defines material properties, e.g. $\vare(\rs)$, the Galerkin Hodge star matrices $\hd_k(\xi), \, k = 1,2$, are given by~\cite{Bossavit1999}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ghs1}
  \brc{\hd_1(\xi)}_{i,j} = \int_{\Omega} \xi\,\Wv{1}_{e_i}\cdot\Wv{1}_{e_j} d\Omega,\quad 1 \leq i,j \leq N_1
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ghs2}
     \brc{\hd_2(\xi)}_{i,j} = \int_{\Omega} \xi\,\Wv{2}_{t_i}\cdot\Wv{2}_{t_j} d\Omega, \quad 1 \leq i,j \leq N_2
\end{equation}
For $k = 0,3$, a diagonal representation of the operators $\hd_0$ and $\hd_3$ is used~\cite{mohamed2016}, and they are given by
\[
 \brc{\hd_0(\xi)}_{i,i} =  \abs{\ast \sigma^0_{i,\xi}},\quad 1\leq i \leq N_0
\]
\[
 \quad \brc{\hd_3(\xi)}_{i,i} = \frac{\xi_i}{\abs{\sigma^3_i}}, \quad 1\leq i \leq N_3
 \]
where $\xi_i = \xi(\rs_i)$ and $r_i$ is the centroid of tetrahedron $\mathcal{T}_i$. The diagonal entry $\abs{\ast \sigma^0_{i,\xi}}$ is the effective dual volume to the node $v_i$ accounting for the variations of the function $\xi$ over the tetrahedra, and is given by
\[
\abs{\ast \sigma^0_{i,\xi}} = \frac{1}{4}\sum_l \xi_l \abs{\sigma^3_{i,l}},
\]
where the sum runs over index $l$ of all tetrahedra sharing the node $v_i$, and the factor $1/4$ accounts for the portion of the tetrahedron volume $\abs{\sigma^3_{i,l}}$ added to the effective dual volume. The following diagram shows how the discrete exterior derivative and Hodge star operators relate the spaces of discrete differential forms for the 3-D case:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:diagram}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large, row sep=large ]
 \CX^0(\mathcal{K}) \arrow{r}{\ded_0} \arrow[d, "\hd_0", shift left=1ex ] & \CX^1(\mathcal{K}) \arrow{r}{\ded_1} \arrow[d, "\hd_1", shift left=1ex ]  & \CX^2(\mathcal{K}) \arrow{r}{\ded_2} \arrow[d, "\hd_2", shift left=1ex ] & \CX^3(\mathcal{K})    \arrow[d, "\hd_3", shift left=1ex ]\\
 \DX^3(\mathbf{\ast} \mathcal{K})  \arrow[u, "\hd_0^{-1}", shift left=1ex ] &  \DX^2(\ast \mathcal{K}) \arrow[l, "-\ded_0^\mathsf{T}"{swap} ] \arrow[u, "\hd_1^{-1}", shift left=1ex ]  &  \DX^1(\ast \mathcal{K}) \arrow[l, "\ded_1^\mathsf{T}"{swap} ] \arrow[u, "\hd_2^{-1}", shift left=1ex ] &  \DX^0(\ast \mathcal{K})   \arrow[l, "-\ded_2^\mathsf{T}"{swap} ] \arrow[u, "\hd_3^{-1}", shift left=1ex ]
\end{tikzcd}
 \end{equation}

To this point, we have introduced all the necessary discrete exterior calculus representations of the exterior calculus operators $\ed$ and $\star$. These aspects will be further explored in Section~\ref{sec5}, where the hybrid approach is developed.

