\section{Threat Model}
\label{secthreatmodel}
In this section, we discuss in-depth how we model the schedule-based attacker and explain our assumptions about the attacker's objectives and capabilities. The objective of the attacker is to utilize the timing information exposed by the processor-level task execution schedules to compromise tasks with higher criticality. 
% \subsection{Schedule-based Attacks}
% \par\noindent 
% We consider a schedule-based attacker model \sd{what does that even mean} in our work, which utilizes the timing information exposed by the processor-level task execution schedules to compromise tasks with higher criticality. 
% employ timing and scheduling aspects of the system to disrupt its operation or compromise its security. 
Since tasks with higher criticality are mostly developed by OEMs or {\em trusted} vendors and go through thorough security scans and functionality checks, they are usually very difficult to compromise~\cite{ren2023protection,schedguard++}. Hence, an attacker is more likely to attempt compromising less critical tasks, that are often assigned lower priorities. We denote such compromisable tasks as {\em untrusted} tasks. Since these untrusted tasks are part of the software that comes from a range of third-party vendors\cite{ren2023protection}, compromising them using a man-in-the-middle-type attack is possible. As mentioned in Sec.~\ref{secIntroduction}, depending on the timing relationship between the victim and attacker task, SBAs can be categorised into 4 types~\cite{schedguard++}. 
\begin{comment}
\textit{(1) Anterior Attack: }An SBA launched \emph{before} the victim task starts its execution is known as an anterior attack.  e.g., \textit{false-data injection} type attacks~\cite{smith2011decoupled,teixeira2015secure,pasqualetti2011cyber,jafarnejadsani2017dual}. \textit{(2) Posterior Attack: }An SBA launched after the victim task completes its execution is known as a posterior attack, e.g., \textit{replay attack, zero-dynamic attack} etc.~\cite{teixeira2012attack,mo2009secure,park2016adversary} \textit{(3) Pincer attack model: }Attack is executed both before and after the victim task~\cite{chen2019novelsidechannel}. \textit{(4) Concurrent attack: }Attack is performed while the victim control task is running and can be launched by executing between the execution window of the victim task’s job~\cite{nathuji2010q,delimitrou2017bolt}.
% Given the potential threat posed by control data override attacks, including bias-injection, zero-dynamics, and replay attacks, we narrow our scope to securing control tasks against posterior schedule-based attacks. Furthermore, 
Embedded controllers are mostly scheduled with fixed-priority preemptive schedulers, which schedule the lower-priority tasks after the higher-priority tasks. 
% a decrease in overall task utilization substantially lowers the probability of a lower-priority task preempting a higher-priority task. 
Since untrusted tasks are mostly of lower priority, CPSs with embedded control software systems are more vulnerable to posterior attacks~\cite{ren2023protection}. 
