The attack-aware schedules generated using the methodology mentioned in Sec.~\ref{subsec:Secure_Sampling_Period_DIscussion} are pre-computed and stored along with their SVIs and victim task-wise AP values after this vulnerability analysis (See Fig.~\ref{fig: Overview_figure}). This process eliminates the need for complex online computations in real-time, thereby minimizing the runtime overhead associated with schedule randomization. 
% Online schedule randomization methodologies introduce considerable runtime overhead~\cite{kruger2018vulnerability}. Therefore, an offline pre-computed schedule selection strategy offers a more efficient approach, provided sufficient memory is available to store all the pre-computed schedules. This offline approach eliminates the need for complex online computations and decision-making in real-time, thereby minimizing the runtime overhead associated with schedule randomization. However, it is crucial to ensure that the embedded system's memory capacity is adequate to accommodate the storage of all the pre-computed schedules, as this is a prerequisite for leveraging the benefits of the offline strategy. It may also be possible that the attacker may have sufficient memory capacity to store all schedules provided it has enough time to observe. However, in our case, the schedules are randomly chosen at the hyper-period boundaries. Therefore, assuming that the attacker has figured out some of the timing properties of the system, very little time remains to launch a directed attack. 
