\section{Related Works}
\label{secRelatedWorks}
Randomization-based defences were proposed as a methodology for defending schedules against schedule-based attacks. TaskShuffler\cite{yoon2016taskshuffler} is one of the state-of-the-art attack mitigation where the author proposes an online algorithm to randomize the task executions while ensuring schedulability. A similar work was proposed in ~\cite{kruger2021randomization}, which randomizes using a slot-shifting technique. However, it has been demonstrated that randomization-based approaches might fail to defend against schedule-based attacks in a few cases since they do not consider attack-effective windows and are attack-type oblivious in nature\cite{chen2022schedguard++}. The authors in ~\cite{} in their work applied window-based protection approaches to prevent posterior side-channel attacks by not executing any untrusted tasks within the attack-effective window of the trusted task. However, such approaches are limited by strong schedulability constraints. They also block untrusted tasks leading to system performance degradation. The authors in ~\cite{} provided initial insights into the online period assignment of control tasks while maintaining their performance. It provides a control scheduling co-design framework that integrates control and scheduling policy for resource optimization. The work in ~\cite{} suggests a method to prevent unstable switching sequences in sampling periods. Instead of optimizing control costs, this approach limits the range of periodicities in a multi-rate control using Lyapunov stability criteria.
