\begin{comment}
Therefore, we introduce a novel metric known as \emph{attack-aware entropy} as a metric to evaluate how robust is a set of random schedules against schedule-based attacks. A lower \emph{attack-aware entropy} value suggests that the schedule set is more vulnerable, while higher values indicate less number of schedule-based attacks.   
We assume that there are total $N$ schedules $s \in \mathcal{S}$. Since sampling rates are varying, Let there be $k$ unique hyper-period lengths and $n_1$, $n_2,..,n_k$ representing hyper-period lengths $l_1, l_2,...,l_k$ respectively.
For a single schedule $s \in S$, length of $s=l$, the attack-aware entropy is given by the following Eq.~\ref{Eq: AAE_single}.
\begin{align}
    \label{Eq: AAE_single}
    H_l(s) = -\sum\limits_{i=1}^m P(atk_i) \log_2 P(atk_i)
\end{align}

\noindent Since the lengths are not static, by taking the weighted average the attack entropy for $H_{l_1}, H_{l_2},..,H_{l_k}$. Therefore, we can represent weighted Attack-Aware Entropy mathematically as:

\begin{align}
\nonumber
H(\mathcal{S}) &= \frac{n_1}{N} \cdot H_{l_1}+ \frac{n_2}{N} \cdot H_{l_2} +...+ \frac{n_k}{N} \cdot H{l_k}\\
&= \frac{1}{N} \Bigr[\sum\limits_{j=1}^k H_{l_j} \cdot n_j \Bigr]\\
&= -\frac{1}{N}\Bigr[\sum\limits_{j=1}^k\sum\limits_{i=1}^m P(atk_i) \log_2 P(atk_i) \times n_j \Bigr]
\end{align}
\begin{align}
    H(S) = \sum\limits_{j=1}^m \sum\limits_{i=1}^n P(atk_i) \log_2 P(atk_i)
\end{align}
Here, $m$ is a variable that denotes the length of the hyper-period of the schedule. We demonstrate this metric for calculating the attack-aware entropy of the below task set. 
