\supplsection{Statistical Tests}

Paired t-test was used when the assumption of normality was satisfied. The formula is given below:

\begin{equation}
t = \frac{{d}}{s_d / \sqrt{n}}
\label{eq:paired_ttest}
\end{equation}


where:

${d}$ is the mean of the differences between paired observations;

$s_d$ is the standard deviation of the differences; and

$n$ is the number of pairs.

\vspace{1em}

For group comparisons in which the normality assumption was not met, we used the Wilcoxon signed-rank test, see Equation \ref{eq:wilcoxon_signed_rank}.


\begin{equation}
W = \min(W^+, W^-)
\label{eq:wilcoxon_signed_rank}
\end{equation}

\noindent
where:

$W^+$ is the sum of positive ranks;

$W^-$ is the sum of negative ranks; and

$m$ is its sample size

\vspace{1em}

Accordingly, the Z-score formula is given below:

\begin{equation}
Z = \frac{W - \mu_W}{\sigma_W}
\end{equation}

where:

\[
\mu_W = \frac{n(n+1)}{4}
\quad \text{and} \quad
\sigma_W = \sqrt{ \frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{24} }
\]


$n$ is the sample size of the groups.

\vspace{1em}

In addition to hypothesis testing, we computed Cohen’s $|d|$ to quantify the effect size and assess the practical relevance of observed differences. Cohen’s $|d|$ is calculated as:

\begin{equation}
|d| = \left| \frac{\mu_1 - \mu_2}{\sqrt{ \frac{s_1^2 + s_2^2}{2} }} \right|
\end{equation}


where:

$\mu_1$ and $\mu_2$ are the means of each group; and

$s_1^2$ and $s_2^2$ are the standard deviations of each group.

