      \emptyset \subset S \subseteq \extens_\alpha, \
       \varphi_\alpha(S) \Rightarrow \lnot\stuck_\alpha, \\
        \ \forall T.\ S \subset T\subseteq \extens_\alpha \Rightarrow \lnot\mathsf{sat}(\varphi_\alpha(T))
        \end{array}
    }
\]
The formula $\stuck_\alpha$ indicates which nodes are guaranteed to encounter a $\bot$ later in their execution.
Extensible nodes $x\in\extens_\alpha$ are not stuck, so there is some computation path that they can take without encountering any $\bot$ node. In (\ref{eq:seq-ex1}), all nodes are extensible, whereas in (\ref{eq:seq-ex2}a) only $x$ and $y_2$ are extensible and in (\ref{eq:seq-ex2}b) none are extensible.
The set of branches $\br_\alpha$ contains all of the maximal formulae that can be obtained as the conjunction of formulae of extensible nodes:
